   "id": "s0",
   "title": "Diamond: a rigid network",
   "text": "In **diamond** each carbon atom is bonded to **four other carbon atoms**, in all directions, forming a **three-dimensional network**.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "sel": 1,
      "sl": 0,
      "cur": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s1",
   "title": "Hard, so useful for cutting",
   "text": "Every bond is strong and there is no way for atoms to slip, so diamond is the **hardest natural substance**: it is used in **drill bits and glass cutters**.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "sel": 1,
      "sl": 0,
      "cur": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s2",
   "title": "Graphite: layers",
   "text": "In **graphite** the carbon atoms are arranged in **layers**. Only **weak forces** hold one layer to the next.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "sel": 2,
      "sl": 0,
      "cur": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "s3",
   "title": "Layers slip",
   "text": "The layers **slide over each other** easily, so graphite is **soft and slippery**: it is used as a **lubricant** and as pencil lead.",
   "actions": [
    {
     "set": {
      "sel": 2,
      "cur": 0
     },
     "at": 0
    },
